# Djot Showcase

This post demonstrates writing a Blogatto post in [djot](https://djot.net/)
instead of Markdown. Djot files use the `.djot` (or `.dj`) extension and are
rendered through the same `Components` configuration as Markdown posts.

## Inline formatting

Djot supports the usual _emphasis_ and *strong*, plus a few extras like
{=highlighted=} text, {-deleted-} text, and {+inserted+} text.

You can also write inline `code` and even reference footnotes inline[^djot].

## Lists

Tight bullet list:

- Parses fast
- Plays nicely with custom components
- Lives next to Markdown posts

Ordered list with a custom start:

3. Third item
4. Fourth item

## Code blocks

```gleam
pub fn hello() -> String {
  "Hello from djot!"
}
```

## Block attributes

Djot lets you annotate any block with attributes using `{...}` syntax: a class
with `{.class-name}`, or arbitrary key/value pairs with `{key="value"}`. These
attributes are passed straight to the matching component, so a theme can react
to them instead of guessing from the content.

This code block carries a `title`, which the `code` component renders as a
caption:

{title="hello.gleam"}
```gleam
pub fn greet() -> String {
  "Hello from a titled code block!"
}
```

## Blockquote

> Djot keeps the readability of Markdown while removing many of its
> ambiguities.

A blockquote annotated with `{.pull}` is turned into a pull-quote by the
`blockquote` component:

{.pull}
> Block attributes make per-element styling possible without smuggling magic
> strings through the prose.

## Link reference

See the [official site][djot-site] for the full specification.

[djot-site]: https://djot.net/

## Footnotes

Footnotes can be referenced anywhere in the text[^one] and their definitions
are rendered at the end of the post[^two]. Multiple footnotes are supported and
appear in a stable order[^three].

[^djot]: Djot was created by John MacFarlane, the author of CommonMark.

[^one]: The first footnote definition.

[^two]: The second footnote, demonstrating that definition bodies are rendered.

[^three]: The third footnote, with some _emphasis_ to show inline formatting
    works inside footnote bodies.
